we're just hearing there from professor  peter openshaw really sounding a warning  that the next few days could be critical  when it comes to suppressing at levels  of the virus  and do you think more restrictions need  to come in  well i think the country will have been  watching with concern  as the r rate was confirmed as over one  on friday and as the number of cases  has risen quite significantly this week  um i think that was  relatively inevitable when the  government were encouraging people to  go out um back into restaurants and pubs  to eat out and to  and to get back um socializing to go  back to work and of course  as millions of children rightly have  returned to school over the last couple  of weeks but what's  really important and what should have  happened to underpin  that returning gradual returning to  normality  was an effective and mass testing system  an effective track and trace system and  the financial ability for people  to self-isolate now we've heard this  week about the many many people that  have struggled to get a test here in  sheffield  over a number of days there wasn't a  single testing appointment  appointment available and i knew of  constituents that were being told  to go into areas that are currently  under lockdown to travel up to scotland  up to the north east  that's completely ridiculous to be  asking people to travel around the  country that are  potentially infected in order to access  tests  so it's um it begs belief really that  when the government had asked people to  get out the house go back to work go  back to school  that they hadn't put in place that  testing capacity in order to underpin it  and to make it safe  that's what's really important now not  necessarily restrictions although we  have supported  the government's return to the rule of  six but in order to make sure that we  underpin a safe  return to work uh and to um slightly  towards normality that we have that  testing regime and track and trace that  underpins it  uh okay and it's interesting to hear you  say that you know you do support  the kind of return to normality if you  like as far as possible so does that  mean that actually  you think the government's got it about  right with opening pubs restaurants  trying to get the economy moving again  well look of course we need to keep the  economy moving and and keeping the  economy moving is not a mutu  it's not at odds with um protecting  people's health because we need  um the economy uh to be functioning in  order to fund the health service and to  make sure that the nhs is as open  as possible but these measures need to  be kept under review  at the moment it appears that  transmission is happening  in households but of course if people  aren't accessing tests  and we know that many many people aren't  you know the fact that there wasn't a  single test  available in sheffield suggests that  it's far greater than the 10 of people  that the government  are suggesting at the moment then the  fact is we don't know exactly where this  transmission is happening  so we need to get that testing system in  place in order to understand exactly  what is happening with the virus  and then all these measures need to be  kept under review we will support the  government  in the in restrictions and in measures  that they take as long as they are based  on evidence and sound medical advice  at the moment we can't know the full  picture because they have not got the  testing capacity in place  interesting to hear about the situation  where you are in sheffield now i want to  move on to brexit because  there's been a lot of movement should we  say on brexit uh in the last week and of  course there'll be even more  next week when the internal market bill  comes to the house of commons now  kirsten has written for the sunday  telegraph uh this morning saying that we  should get on with brexit so we can  focus on defeating coronavirus  i mean it's pretty easy to throw around  these slogans isn't it get on with  brexit let's get brexit done  but i'm just interested in what that  actually means  is labour effectively saying then that  the government should compromise  on the two outstanding issues that  preventing a trade deal right now so  would labour  support the government rolling over  on fishing and also on state aid  well um kierson as you say has written  for the telegraph to say that  boris johnson's actions and the  government's actions this week  completely compromised progress towards  securing a free trade  deal with the european union what  they've done by picking up you know that  the things that are  that we know uh forgive me that some of  the things that are holding up the trade  deal  are on fishing and on state aid so in  order to get brexit done is labour  saying that the government should  compromise on those two things  well the point is sophie that these  issues are not insignificant and they're  important they're important to slaves of  our economy but they're certainly not  insurmountable  and what's baffling about the  government's approach is that it there  was every  um every prospect that those issues were  going to reach agreement in the joint  committee this week  and it's really important that it is  through that joint committee between the  uk  and the eu that that agreement is  reached that was the that was what was  put forward in the withdrawal agreement  and indeed in the northern ireland  protocol what would say on state aid  is this the government has not used its  ability to spend  on state aid even whilst we've remained  in the eu  so to the idea that we're picking a  fight with the european union on this  and potentially undermining the prospect  of  securing a trade deal is quite frankly  baffling when they not even  use their the ability and their  potential to to spend on state aid  whilst we've been a member of the  european union  look boris johnson's and his  government's actions are seriously  undermining  and jeopardizing the chance of securing  a free trade agreement with the european  union it's unilateral approach it's easy  to criticize the government and the  negotiating tactics and certainly you  know when we talk to robert buckland  we'll be going over whether or not this  was a  sensible move for the government to do  but i just want to go back to the  fundamental  point because it's very easy to say  let's get brexit done let's get a trade  deal  the two outstanding issues are on state  aid  subsidies and on fishing and i still  just don't understand whether or not  labour would compromise on those two  things  well sophie the protocol states that  state aid will apply  um to northern ireland that is what the  pro that is that is where the agreement  um that is what that was what was agreed  that was what was negotiated that was  what was written down and ratified into  international treaty  by boris johnson that has already been  agreed that so you're happy then with  the  existing deal even though you voted  against it you said it was a betrayal  we didn't the reason we voted against it  wasn't because of the northern ireland  protocol we have always supported  measures in order to protect  the good friday agreement and to avoid a  border on the island of ireland  the protocol was designed in order to  uphold that good friday agreement and to  preserve  peace and prosperity in northern ireland  we didn't oppose  theresa may's deal on the basis of the  backstop nor did we  oppose boris johnson's deal on the basis  of the protocol  what what his actions now are doing is  undermining  the protocol and necessarily pushing the  conversation  back to the border on the island of  ireland that is seriously  um risky and undermines all the progress  that has been made  since the good friday agreement so  kierst almost says he wants to you know  stop banging on  about europe labour now saying that the  government shouldn't be rowing back and  trying to override a withdrawal  agreement that you voted against and  thought was a bad deal at the time  isn't the truth of it that actually  labour is really acting in its own  self-interest here you just want to stop  talking about brexit because you know  that that would be the best thing for  you politically  well i think the country wants to stop  talking about um brexit um you know and  the people of northern ireland  um many different people across  communities that i've spoken  to are looking upon this incredibly  wearily  that these issues that we were told were  settled are now being reopened that  wounds that we thought were healing  and now being reopened and that the  government are unnecessarily inflicting  this  again on the country and quite frankly  this is the last thing the country needs  right now  as we're attempting to respond to covid  and cope with economic recovery  what we need is a deal with the european  union that protects  our trading relationship protects  businesses protects jobs and protects  peace in northern ireland what boris  johnson is doing  is risking all of that and it's as i say  the very last thing the country needs at  this critical moment  you know we've left the european union  on the 31st of december the  transposition period will end  we'll be stepping out onto the world  stage on the 1st of january  as global britain for the first time in  40 years as  non-members of the european union why on  earth  would we be stepping into that next  chapter of our history as the united  kingdom  undermining our reputation as a country  that respects its word  and upholds the international rule of  law okay  now we are out of time but i just want  to ask you just very quickly  uh to comment on the government's new  sentencing  uh policy so at the minute you can only  get a whole life sentence if you're over  21  they want to say that if you're between  18 and 21  you should be able to get a whole life  sentence is that something that labor  supports  well look we'll um we'll review the law  when it comes forward and we'll consider  um whether or not to support it then but  uh you know and i hope that robert  buckland  addresses this later i think what's  important to note is that the criminal  justice system  is in complete disarray because of um  years of  cuts of privatization the probation  system and of undermining  of the courts and of the police so you  know there are many issues with the  criminal justice system  the length of sentencing is is quite low  down  the list of issues that faces it at the  moment

